\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {2}A crude device for measuring charge consists of two small insulating spheres of radius $ a $, one of which is fixed in position. The other is movable along the x-axis and is subject to a restraining force $ kx $, where $ k $ is a spring constant. The uncharged spheres are centered at $ x = 0$ and $ x = d $, the latter fixed. If the spheres are given equal and opposite charges of $ \frac  {Q}{C} $, obtain the expression by which $ Q $ may be found as a function of $ x$.Determine the maximum charge that can be measured in terms of $ \epsilon _0 $, $ k $, and $ d $, and state the separation of the spheres. What happens if a larger charge is applied?}{5}{section.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:my_label}{{2}{5}{A crude device for measuring charge consists of two small insulating spheres of radius $ a $, one of which is fixed in position. The other is movable along the x-axis and is subject to a restraining force $ kx $, where $ k $ is a spring constant. The uncharged spheres are centered at $ x = 0$ and $ x = d $, the latter fixed. If the spheres are given equal and opposite charges of $ \frac {Q}{C} $, obtain the expression by which $ Q $ may be found as a function of $ x$.Determine the maximum charge that can be measured in terms of $ \epsilon _0 $, $ k $, and $ d $, and state the separation of the spheres. What happens if a larger charge is applied?}{section.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {3}A flux density field is given as\begin {linenomath}\[\mathbf  {F_1} = 5 \hat  {a}_z.\]\end  {linenomath}The task is to evaluate the outward flux of \( \mathbf  {F_1} \) through the hemispherical surface defined by \( r = a \),\begin {linenomath}\[0 < \theta < \frac  {\pi }{2}, \hskip 1em\relax 0 < \phi < 2\pi .\]\end  {linenomath}Next, consider what simple observation would have saved a lot of work in the previous part. Identifying symmetries or using alternative methods can simplify the calculations significantly. Now suppose the field is given by \begin {linenomath}\[\mathbf  {F_2} = 5z \hat  {a}_z.\]\end  {linenomath}Using the appropriate surface integrals, determine the net outward flux of \( \mathbf  {F_2} \) through the closed surface consisting of the hemisphere from the first part and its circular base in the \( xy \)-plane. Finally, repeat the previous calculation by applying the divergence theorem and evaluating an appropriate volume integral. This approach should confirm the result obtained through direct surface integration.}{8}{section.3}\protected@file@percent }
